A rustic Southern comfort food classic that comes together in one skillet with minimal fuss. Smoky, savory diced ham is browned in a blend of butter and olive oil, then slowly braised down with sweet onions and a mountain of green cabbage until delightfully tender and caramelized.

*Adapted from [Foodie Chicks Rule](https://foodiechicksrule.com/fried-cabbage-ham/).*

## Ingredients

- 1 medium head green cabbage, cored and chopped into 1-inch bite-sized pieces
- 1 large sweet onion (such as Vidalia or Walla Walla), chopped into 1-inch pieces
- 1 small bone-in or boneless ham steak (approx. 8 to 10 oz), cut into ½-inch dice
- 2 tablespoons extra-virgin olive oil
- 2 tablespoons unsalted butter
- ½ teaspoon freshly cracked black pepper
- Kosher salt, to taste
- ¼ teaspoon smoked paprika or garlic powder (optional)

## Instructions

1. **Prep the Produce and Meat:** Peel off and discard any wilted outer leaves from the cabbage. Cut the head into quarters, slice out the dense core from each quarter, and chop into rough 1-inch bite-sized squares. Dice the sweet onion into similar 1-inch chunks. Trim any excess rind from the ham steak and dice the meat into ½-inch cubes.
2. **Brown the Diced Ham:** Set a large, deep 12-inch cast iron skillet or Dutch oven over medium heat. Add the olive oil and butter. Once the butter foams, add the diced ham steak. Sauté for 3 to 4 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the ham edges turn lightly golden and fragrant.
3. **Incorporate Cabbage and Onions:** Add the chopped sweet onion and pile the chopped cabbage into the skillet. If your pan seems overflowing, cover with a lid for 2 to 3 minutes to steam and collapse the cabbage down slightly, then remove the lid and toss well to coat every shred in the ham drippings, butter, and oil.
4. **Sauté to Caramelized Tenderness:** Cook uncovered over medium heat for 20 to 25 minutes, stirring every few minutes. Allow the cabbage to sit against the bottom of the hot pan between stirs so it develops sweet, golden-brown caramelized patches. The dish is done when the cabbage is thoroughly wilted, silky, and tender.
5. **Season and Serve:** Taste a bite of cabbage before adding salt, since the cured ham releases natural saltiness into the dish. Season with black pepper, a pinch of kosher salt if desired, and smoked paprika or garlic powder if using. Spoon onto plates and serve steaming hot.

## Notes

- **Leftover Holiday Ham:** This recipe is an ideal vehicle for leftover holiday spiral-sliced or baked glazed ham.
- **Keto / Low-Carb Friendly:** Naturally low in carbohydrates and rich in fiber, making it a satisfying standalone dinner or side dish alongside cornbread.
